{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"My dear Marie,\n\nParis still holds me. Yesterday I saw the Englishman Shakespeare performed. Kemble as Romeo is dignified, but the play itself disappoints. They keep Juliet awake while Romeo is still alive – an unnecessary hesitation that dilutes the death. And then this custom: Romeo sits down next to Juliet before he speaks a word. It is a ridiculous confusion of physical proximity and ethical connection. Love is not mere contact, but a recognition of the spirit in the Other. Mercutio chatters with the Nurse while the lovers remain silent? That is comedy where tragedy is required. We Germans know the inner necessity better. I drink my wine, think of the order of our affairs at home, and am grateful that our sons in Berlin are learning the disciplined seriousness that is so often lacking here.\n\nYour faithful Wilhelm"}}],"created":1795221209,"model":"qwen","system_fingerprint":"b11429-d81235049","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":167,"prompt_tokens":278,"total_tokens":445,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-ji1bGPjQ5taWMx61wGyPBQqRYMbNxSpD","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":278,"prompt_ms":384.631,"prompt_per_token_ms":1.3835647482014388,"prompt_per_second":722.7706555113863,"predicted_n":167,"predicted_ms":3879.65,"predicted_per_token_ms":23.371385542168674,"predicted_per_second":42.78736483961182}}